        "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/jimvixx/subscriptions",
        "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/jimvixx/orgs",
        "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/jimvixx/repos",
        "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/jimvixx/events{/privacy}",
        "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/jimvixx/received_events",
        "type": "User",
        "user_view_type": "public",
        "site_admin": false
      },
      "created_at": "2026-10-17T17:02:51Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-10-17T17:02:51Z",
      "body": "Thank you for the contribution and for testing SMSecure with RVSec!\r\n\r\nI reviewed the proposed change. According to the Android documentation, `HmacSHA1` is a supported `KeyGenerator` algorithm, so the reported violation may be related to the CrySL rule rather than an actual Android API issue.\r\n\r\nAlso, the generated key material is subsequently used with `HmacSHA1` throughout the existing MasterSecret implementation. Switching only the key generator to `HmacSHA256` does not change the MAC algorithm or provide a direct security improvement.\r\n\r\nCould you share the exact CrySL rule violation and explain why `HmacSHA1` is considered invalid in this context?\r\n\r\nI'd prefer to preserve the existing cryptographic behavior unless there is a demonstrated issue. Adding an explicit 160-bit initialization to the current `HmacSHA1` generator might be a simpler alternative.\r\n\r\nThanks again for investigating this!",
      "author_association": "OWNER",
      "reactions": {
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/jimvixx/ShortMessageSecure/issues/comments/6237208144/reactions",
        "total_count": 0,
